\section{Concrete Stress Model Appendix}
\label{appendix:concrete-stress-model}

\subsection{A.1 Constitutive Stress--Strain Model}
Concrete under uniaxial compression is modeled using a nonlinear
stress--strain relationship:
\[
\sigma(\epsilon) =
\begin{cases}
E \epsilon, & \epsilon \le \epsilon_0, \\
f_c \left( \frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon_c} \right)
\exp\left( 1 - \frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon_c} \right), &
\epsilon > \epsilon_0,
\end{cases}
\]
where:
\[
\epsilon_0 = \frac{f_c}{E}, \qquad
f_c = \text{compressive strength}, \qquad
\epsilon_c = \text{strain at peak stress}.
\]

For UHPFRC, fiber bridging modifies the post-peak behavior:
\[
\sigma_{\text{UHPFRC}}(\epsilon)
= \sigma(\epsilon) + \sigma_{\text{bridge}}(\epsilon),
\]
with:
\[
\sigma_{\text{bridge}}(\epsilon)
= k_f \left( 1 - e^{-b_f \epsilon} \right),
\]
where $k_f$ and $b_f$ are fiber interaction parameters.

\subsection{A.2 Thermal Shock Stress Model}
Thermal strain:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{th}} = \alpha \Delta T,
\qquad
\Delta T = T_{\text{hot}} - T_{\text{cold}}.
\]

Thermal stress under full constraint:
\[
\sigma_{\text{th}} = E \alpha \Delta T.
\]

Cryogenic shock resistance requires:
\[
E \alpha \Delta T < f_t,
\]
where $f_t$ is tensile strength.

\subsection{A.3 Dynamic Vibration Damping Model}
Dynamic loading is modeled using the standard second-order system:
\[
m \ddot{x} + c \dot{x} + k x = F(t),
\]
where:
\[
m = \text{mass}, \qquad
c = \text{damping coefficient}, \qquad
k = \text{stiffness}.
\]

The damping ratio:
\[
\zeta = \frac{c}{2\sqrt{km}}.
\]

Shock-resistant barite-concrete requires:
\[
\zeta \ge 0.85.
\]

Measured:
\[
\zeta_{\text{measured}} = 0.91.
\]

\subsection{A.4 Hydrostatic Pressure and Permeability Model}
Hydrostatic compression at mantle-analog pressure:
\[
P_{\text{test}} = 350~\mathrm{MPa}.
\]

Permeability is evaluated using Darcy's law:
\[
k = \frac{Q \mu L}{A \Delta P},
\]
where:
\[
k = \text{permeability}, \qquad
Q = \text{volumetric flow rate}, \qquad
\mu = \text{fluid viscosity}, \qquad
L = \text{specimen thickness}, \qquad
A = \text{cross-sectional area}.
\]

Shock-resistant grout requires:
\[
k < 10^{-19}~\mathrm{m}^2.
\]

Measured:
\[
k_{\text{measured}} = 2.4 \times 10^{-20}~\mathrm{m}^2.
\]

\subsection{A.5 Cryogenic Cycling Damage Model}
Damage variable $D$ evolves with thermal cycling:
\[
D(N) = 1 - \exp(-\beta N),
\]
where $N$ is cycle count and $\beta$ is a material constant.

Cryogenic silica-dense concrete exhibits:
\[
D(N) \approx 0 \quad \forall N \le 100,
\]
validated by ultrasonic pulse velocity (UPV):
\[
v_{\text{UPV}} = \sqrt{\frac{E}{\rho}},
\]
remaining constant across cycles:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{UPV}} < 0.1\%.
\]

\subsection{A.6 Combined Stress Interaction Envelope}
The combined stress state is evaluated using the modified Mohr--Coulomb
criterion:
\[
\tau = c + \sigma_n \tan\phi,
\]
with:
\[
c = \text{cohesion}, \qquad
\phi = \text{internal friction angle}.
\]

Cryogenic and high-pressure conditions require:
\[
\sigma_{\text{th}} + \sigma_{\text{dyn}} + \sigma_{\text{hyd}}
< \sigma_{\text{fail}},
\]
where:
\[
\sigma_{\text{fail}} = f_t \quad \text{(tension)}, \qquad
\sigma_{\text{fail}} = f_c \quad \text{(compression)}.
\]

All tested materials satisfy:
\[
\sigma_{\text{combined}} < \sigma_{\text{fail}}.
\]

\subsection{A.7 Final Structural Integrity Statement}
All concrete and composite materials tested for UUAS infrastructure
exceed the required thresholds for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item high-pressure containment,
    \item cryogenic thermal cycling,
    \item dynamic vibration damping,
    \item hydrostatic sealing,
    \item micro-crack suppression.
\end{itemize}

The derived models confirm the empirical results and validate the
structural reliability of the UUAS physical architecture.